[38;5;231m⏺[39m Validation only. One more constraint question before I lay out approaches — this one is forced by the repo being
  split.

  [1mThe fork:[0m [38;5;153mapp.js[39m loads as a plain [38;5;153m<script>[39m in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m with no module system, while [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m uses CommonJS
  [38;5;153mrequire[39m. A shared validation module has to be reachable from browser form code, and there's no bundler and no build
  step here.

  - [1mES modules[0m — change [38;5;153mindex.html[39m to [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m and use [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m. Modern, no build step, works in
    every current browser. Costs: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m becomes a module (file:// loading now needs a local server), and it won't
    [38;5;153mrequire()[39m from the existing CommonJS [38;5;153msrc/[39m without care.
  - [1mPlain script + global[0m — load [38;5;153mvalidation.js[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, expose [38;5;153mwindow.Validation[39m. Zero friction, matches what's
    there today. Costs: no import graph, awkward to unit-test in Node, and it's the thing you'd migrate away from later.
  - [1mCommonJS + add a bundler[0m — consistent with [38;5;153msrc/[39m, testable in Node directly. Costs: introduces a build step and a
    dependency to a repo that currently has neither.

  Expensive to change later: none of these badly — the validator's [3mrules format[0m is the sticky part, not its module
  wrapper. But switching module systems does touch every file that imports it.

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules.[0m It's the only option that's both build-step-free and testable, and the repo is small
  enough that converting [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a two-line change.
